\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Pelvic anatomical framework, morphometric dimensions, boundary conditions, and standardized loading regimes.} (A) 3D finite-element rendering of the human pelvis showing the bilateral sacroiliac joint (SIJ) articular cartilages (orange), the superior S1 sacral facet constrained via a Dirichlet penalty ($\mathbf  {u} = \mathbf  {0}, \gamma = 10^6\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N/mm}$; green), and the anterior fibrocartilaginous pubic symphysis disc (purple). Overlays mark the primary morphometric triad: anteroposterior inlet diameter (conjugate vera $d_{\text  {AP}}$, blue), biischiadic interspinous width ($w_{\text  {biisch}}$, amber), and subpubic arch angle ($\alpha _{\text  {subpubic}}$, magenta). (B) Locomotor stance configurations and boundary conditions: symmetrical two-leg stance (SP2leg; $+400\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N}$ per acetabular notch, $+800\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N}$ total vertical resultant reacting against the constrained sacral base) and single-leg stance (SP1leg; $+800\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N}$ unilateral vertical ground reaction on the right acetabulum with unsupported contralateral hemi-pelvis). (C) Parturition-motivated mechanical expansion proxies: LAB1 (internal pelvic ring compression/shear proxy; $\pm 400\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N}$ mediolateral pair on pectineal surfaces), LAB2 (ischial tuberosity transverse distraction proxy; $\pm 400\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N}$ outward mediolateral pair), and LAB3 (outlet anteroposterior distraction proxy; $-400\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N}$ anterior pubis, $+400\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\text  {N}$ posterior sacrococcygeal joint). (D) Applied resultant force components across anatomical axes: mediolateral (ML, green), anteroposterior (AP, blue), and craniocaudal (CC, vermilion). Mathematical formulations for the continuum boundary value problem, landmark extractions, rigid kinematic registration, and local Cardan angle extractions are detailed in Appendix~\ref {app:mathematical_formulation}.}}{11}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{11}{\textbf {Pelvic anatomical framework, morphometric dimensions, boundary conditions, and standardized loading regimes.} (A) 3D finite-element rendering of the human pelvis showing the bilateral sacroiliac joint (SIJ) articular cartilages (orange), the superior S1 sacral facet constrained via a Dirichlet penalty ($\mathbf {u} = \mathbf {0}, \gamma = 10^6\;\text {N/mm}$; green), and the anterior fibrocartilaginous pubic symphysis disc (purple). Overlays mark the primary morphometric triad: anteroposterior inlet diameter (conjugate vera $d_{\text {AP}}$, blue), biischiadic interspinous width ($w_{\text {biisch}}$, amber), and subpubic arch angle ($\alpha _{\text {subpubic}}$, magenta). (B) Locomotor stance configurations and boundary conditions: symmetrical two-leg stance (SP2leg; $+400\;\text {N}$ per acetabular notch, $+800\;\text {N}$ total vertical resultant reacting against the constrained sacral base) and single-leg stance (SP1leg; $+800\;\text {N}$ unilateral vertical ground reaction on the right acetabulum with unsupported contralateral hemi-pelvis). (C) Parturition-motivated mechanical expansion proxies: LAB1 (internal pelvic ring compression/shear proxy; $\pm 400\;\text {N}$ mediolateral pair on pectineal surfaces), LAB2 (ischial tuberosity transverse distraction proxy; $\pm 400\;\text {N}$ outward mediolateral pair), and LAB3 (outlet anteroposterior distraction proxy; $-400\;\text {N}$ anterior pubis, $+400\;\text {N}$ posterior sacrococcygeal joint). (D) Applied resultant force components across anatomical axes: mediolateral (ML, green), anteroposterior (AP, blue), and craniocaudal (CC, vermilion). Mathematical formulations for the continuum boundary value problem, landmark extractions, rigid kinematic registration, and local Cardan angle extractions are detailed in Appendix~\ref {app:mathematical_formulation}}{figure.caption.4}{}}
